<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ninja">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ninja</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shinobi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ninja, or shinobi, were warriors of pre-modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> who specialised in espionage, scouting, infiltration and surprise attacks, especially in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> of the 15th and 16th centuries. The best-known came from the provinces of Iga and Kōka, whose warrior leagues hired them out to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>. Under the Tokugawa some served as guards and spies, and later legend greatly embellished their image.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ninja.jpg" alt="Ninja" width="320" height="593" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Katsushika Hokusai; derivative work by AMorozov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hokusai-sketches---hokusai-manga-vol6-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ninja is the on'yomi reading of the two characters 忍者, read in the native kun'yomi as shinobi, a shortened form of shinobi-no-mono. The word shinobi appears as early as the late 8th century in poems of the Man'yōshū, and means to steal away or hide.</p><p>Historical accounts of the ninja are scarce, despite many folktales. Later tradition gives the title retrospectively to Yamato Takeru, a prince of the 2nd century, and says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-shotoku">Prince Shōtoku</a> employed a spy named Otomono Sahito, though evidence for him is thin. Only from the 15th century were spies specially trained, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> documents began to call stealthy soldiers shinobi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> shinobi served as spies, scouts, surprise attackers and agitators. Ninja families were organised into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> with their own territories and a system of rank. The jōnin, or upper person, was the highest, representing the group and hiring out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>; below were the chūnin, his assistants, and the genin, field agents drawn from the lower class who carried out the missions.</p><p>The best-known ninja came from the province of Iga and the adjacent region of Kōka. Their families were jizamurai, peasant-warriors who in the unrest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> formed ikki, leagues for self-defence, and sold their services as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and spies. The remote mountains of Iga may have favoured their secretive development.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Daimyōs hired professional ninja between 1485 and 1581, and Iga ninja were sought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-warfare">siege warfare</a>, including night attacks and ambushes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> forced the Kōka ikki to become his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> in 1574 and in 1581 invaded Iga and wiped out the organised clans. Survivors fled, some to the mountains of Kii and others to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, who treated them well, and Hattori Hanzō and other former Iga members later served as his bodyguards. Tradition holds that Iga and Kōka ninja helped Ieyasu escape through Iga after Nobunaga's death, though researchers at Mie University suggest it was Kōka jizamurai militias rather than ninja, and the scholar Tatsuo Fujita doubts the account of Hanzō's ninja army.</p><p>Under the Tokugawa a ninja is said to have planted the besiegers' flag on the gate at the Siege of Hataya in 1600, and Iga warriors saw action at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> in 1614. The last detailed record of ninja in open warfare comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimabara-rebellion">Shimabara Rebellion</a> of 1637 to 1638, when Kōga ninja were recruited against the Christian rebels at Hara Castle. After the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> in the 17th century the ninja faded into obscurity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ninja” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Men of Iga were hired across Japan as specialists in scouting, infiltration and raids, the <b>shinobi</b>, or ninja.</blockquote><a href="/state/iga-confederacy">Iga confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Fighting with <b>Ninja</b> rebels in the Pool region in 2016 ended in a ceasefire in December 2017.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a></li></ul></section>
